items=[('Stereotypy has an individual history.','Repeated movement depends on current opportunities, social exposure and prior transfers; record its timing and functional context.'),('Stress coordinates several outputs.','Mouse CRH neurons control endocrine and behavioral responses through distinct routes, including rapid glutamatergic hypothalamic transmission.'),('Endocrine measurements have a time scale.','Fecal metabolites integrate earlier secretion and are interpreted with season, sampling method and each elephant’s reference record.'),('Usable choice changes experience.','Distributed feeding, compatible social access and suitable resting surfaces support different actions and individual decisions.'),('Multiple outcomes describe adaptation.','Activity, recumbence, hormone patterns and social behavior can change differently during a habitat intervention or reintegration.'),('Neuroethical care follows lived outcomes.','Evaluate behavioral opportunities, nutrition, health and social conditions together, and follow each elephant’s response to an intervention.')]
